+    #
+    # NO max_server_memory_usage KEY LIVES HERE, and its absence is deliberate. Two pull requests
+    # put one in (#3349 at 3435973836, #3361 at 4187593113) and both were dead letters: ClickHouse
+    # ignores it. `config.xml` pins `<max_server_memory_usage>0</max_server_memory_usage>` -- zero
+    # meaning AUTO -- and the auto value is recomputed from `max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio`
+    # 0.9 at every load, so the ratio always wins. Proved on the live pod: the operator's own
+    # `chop-generated-settings.xml` carried 4187593113 and `system.server_settings` still reported
+    # 3865470566, the 0.9 x 4Gi auto figure. The ratio setting cannot be changed at runtime and
+    # the chart renders no `files` key, so it is not reachable from this repository at all.
+    #
+    # Worse, the 3.20 GiB ceiling in #3349 briefly DID take effect and denied service. ClickHouse's
+    # own config.xml says why, in a comment on the parameter itself: "It is not practical to set
+    # this constraint to small values like just a few gigabytes, because memory allocator will keep
+    # this amount of memory in caches and the server will deny service of queries." Measured:
+    #
+    #   Code: 241 (MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED): would use 1.65 GiB, current RSS: 3.25 GiB,
+    #   maximum: 3.20 GiB -- while executing AggregatingTransform.
+    #
+    # on `select table, count() parts ... from system.parts group by table`. ClickHouse could not
+    # aggregate over its own part list, so the diagnostic query needed to investigate the outage
+    # was itself refused. A ceiling is not the lever. Resident parts are.
+    #
+    # The settings below are the ones that measurably helped.
